\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
A Cartan--Hadamard manifold is complete, simply connected, and has nonpositive sectional curvature. In dimension $n$, must every bounded sufficiently regular domain $\Omega$ in such a manifold satisfy the sharp Euclidean isoperimetric bound
\[|\partial\Omega|\geq n\omega_n^{1/n}|\Omega|^{(n-1)/n},\]
where $\omega_n$ is the volume of the Euclidean unit ball?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.3115}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
A Cartan--Hadamard manifold is complete, simply connected, and has nonpositive sectional curvature. In dimension $n$, must every bounded sufficiently regular domain $\Omega$ in such a manifold satisfy the sharp Euclidean isoperimetric bound
\[|\partial\Omega|\geq n\omega_n^{1/n}|\Omega|^{(n-1)/n},\]
where $\omega_n$ is the volume of the Euclidean unit ball?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1205 (GEO-014). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.3115}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:1205}

