\section*{The Cartan-Hadamard Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Model isoperimetry and equality rigidity}: The manuscript claims sharp comparison with the constant-curvature model ball for every finite-volume finite-perimeter set in a simply connected complete manifold whose sectional curvatures do not exceed $\kappa\le0$; in the Euclidean comparison, bounded equality sets are precisely regions isometric to round Euclidean balls, up to null sets.

\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: The paper claims the Euclidean-optimal integral filling inequality for compactly supported integer cycles of dimension at least two in proper CAT(0) spaces; arbitrary integer multiplicities and ambient dimensions are permitted.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Model isoperimetry and equality rigidity}: A sharp critical Sobolev inequality is established on immersed hypersurfaces using radial sphere maps and positive weights compatible with Jacobi comparison. This controls the boundary geometry needed for the isoperimetric estimate. Approximation extends the bound to finite-perimeter sets, and the equality analysis forces the geometric comparison to be exact, identifying a Euclidean ball region.

\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: Induction reduces a failing inequality to a compact CAT(0) space and an extremizing cycle of small normalized mass. Inward geodesic variations estimate its curvature-like behavior in current charts. The resulting sharp mass comparison contradicts extremality, and Euclidean examples show that the coefficient cannot be improved.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Model isoperimetry and equality rigidity}: The sharp model theorem contains the entire Euclidean Cartan-Hadamard inequality and additionally supplies negative-curvature-model comparisons and Euclidean equality rigidity.

\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: Sharp integral fillings in proper CAT(0) spaces imply the smooth Hadamard-domain isoperimetric bound for ambient dimensions at least three; the two-dimensional bound is the classical case explicitly included in the paper. Equality rigidity is not part of these B statements.

The claimed result implies the stated question and also supplies the additional strength described above.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 337 describes Lean coverage for the sharp integral-current CAT(0) filling estimate and its optimal coefficient; the curved-model perimeter theorem is separate.

\textit{Model isoperimetry and equality rigidity}: No manuscript-specific Comparator is listed in the pinned inventory.

The documented selection does not cover the entire manuscript assertion relevant here.

\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: The manuscript links \texttt{FillingCoefficient.lean}, \texttt{SharpCAT0Filling.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

